A||N||9||Jefrysuko||Performance Mod from Fiero-List||||1||
Z||000000||Jefrysuko||06-02-2001||10:23 PM||||Hey guys this came off the Fiero-List.  Just wondering what your thoughts are.  He is claiming a half second gain in 0-60 times.<P><BR><B>Date: Sat, 02 Jun 2001 11:57:04 -0500<BR>From: "Andy Bejcek" &lt;bejcek@shianet.org&gt;<BR>To: fiero-list@fiero.org<BR>Subject: Re: (Fiero) V-6 Performance<P>The mod that I have been talking about is a set of spacers for the V6 Fiero<BR>intake manifold.  I have the resources to produce an entire kit that solves<BR>all problems encountered when doing this.  Like I said before, I've had the<BR>spacers on my personal car for 1,000+ miles with no problems.  The basic<BR>idea is that longer intake manifold runners increase low end/mid range<BR>torque.  The reason why I want to do some more testing is because I am a<BR>little concerned that 1/4 mile times might be harmed. Personally, I was not<BR>expecting such a difference in 0-60 times, I just figured that every little<BR>bit would help me in the end. Expect to learn more in the upcoming weeks.<P>Please give me your opinion/thoughts,<P>Andy</B><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeffrey D Suckow<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:Jefrysuko@aol.com">Jefrysuko@aol.com</A>  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Zippy_11.jpg">                    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT_4.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||c m west||06-02-2001||11:39 PM||||from an engineering stand point there would be no sufficient gain by lengthening the intake runners with spacers, seeing as how GM did this by the inherent design of the intake setup ( each runner nearly a foot long), believe he just wants to make a sale<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||06-03-2001||07:46 AM||||Reminds me of the time I added a 2 inch spacer under my 4bbl Holley and dropped a second off my quarter mile time...<BR>Donno what will happen till you try it...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||TRiAD||06-03-2001||10:21 AM||||*scratching my head*<BR>Well, in theory, it would work. But I dunno....<BR>So, let's all attatch garden hoses to each of out intake runners, and coil them up in the trunk!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bwaaahahahaha!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com<BR>-----------------<BR>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||5
Z||000004||88formula||06-03-2001||11:25 AM||||It is true that longer runners increase low-end torque but usually hurt top-end power. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||06-03-2001||09:53 PM||||It's not that simple.<BR>The runner length <I>and</I> valve duration together determine resonant RPM.<P>I can believe it if peak torque drops from 3500 to 3000 RPM, while peak power stays at 4500 RPM -&gt; a lot more powerband to play with.<BR>The new M3 and the 911(996) Turbo both have a few things to say about powerband width.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||06-04-2001||02:36 PM||||i shaved nearly 2" off a spare intake Shaun41178 and I have.. and gain hp but the torque just plunged... and it's got NASTY harmonic problems at 2800-3500rpm chokes and hic-ups and bucks.. and then BOOM.. it's pulling like a train past redline... D@MN i need to scan those dyno sheets... <P>1/2 a second is TUFF to gain... a stout nitrous shot will takes big time off not so sure about the intake being that dramatic... ask for dyno proof. there is a dyno nearly everywhere... <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  look in the locater... ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Toddster||06-04-2001||08:17 PM||||I don't know where CM got his BS degree but I got mine at Georgia Tech and there is a very sound engineering rationale for the increase in performance. Not so much for the increase in length as for the shape of the runners &lt;i&gt;due to&lt;/i&gt; the increased length. It will invariably increase air velocity and that can have an impact of low end torque. <P>But without knowing how much the runners have been lengthened, whether or not other mods have been performed, and without seeing a Before-and-after dyno test, I have to say, "show me the money" before I break out the hacksaw.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jefrysuko||06-04-2001||08:43 PM||||Here is an update guy's.<P><B>Date: Mon, 04 Jun 2001 16:06:21 -0500<BR>From: "Andy Bejcek" &lt;bejcek@shianet.org&gt;<BR>To: fiero-list@fiero.org<BR>Subject: (Fiero) Intake Spacers (V-6 mod)<BR>Message-Id: &lt;200106042004.f54K4FR24393@dilbert.communist.org&gt;<BR>Content-type: text/plain; charset="US-ASCII"<BR>Content-transfer-encoding: 7bit<P>I guess I had forgotten what the list is like... you're not all greedy <BR>capitalists like me!  I didn't know other people have considered this idea.<BR>I was nervous about mentioning it because it was one of my friend's ideas.<BR>I thought he might be a little mad if I let out the "secret" this way.<BR>Anyhow, looks like you'll get the full story now.<P>The spacers I'm working on go between the upper and middle intake manifold.<BR>They are 1" thick, and made out of a nylon/teflon-like plastic.  Before you<BR>all e-mail me, asking how plastic can survive in the engine compartment, I<BR>think you should know some more about it.  This plastic can withstand<BR>temperatures over 200 degrees F.  I know for a fact that the spacers do fine<BR>under any driving conditions.<P>There are problems with lifting the intake, but not many.  The TB heating<BR>lines are attached to the upper intake, so it just needs longer hoses where<BR>it meets up with the thermostat housing (these would be included in the<BR>kit). Also, the PCV line that enters the TB hose will no longer line up.  I<BR>have bent mine into place, since it is made out of very flexible tubing.  I<BR>wish there were a neater way to do this, but bending the tube is about as<BR>good as I can do.<P>Obviously longer bolts are required.  I'm not going to be able to find<BR>chrome 12-points like the originals, and I would probably include what I'm<BR>using now - internal hex drive bolts.  If the kit included these bolts, I<BR>would also include the correct size 3/8 drive socket.<P>Two to four new gaskets are needed, depending on whether the old ones are<BR>re-used.  I found that the only way to buy the gaskets individually is<BR>through GM.  I still get screwed on the price, but it's better than buying a<BR>whole kit.<P>Now, for performance.  Over the winter, using several base runs and several<BR>"modified" runs for an example, I determined that the 0-60 performance gain<BR>was approximately .5 to 1 second.  This was using a G-Tech Pro on my 85 SE<BR>V6 Auto.  I did not hold gears, but the trans already holds them to a few<BR>hundered RPM off of the redline (5500-5800 RPM).  There are only two<BR>non-factory items on my car - a 180 degree thermostat and a K&N (yeah,yeah,<BR>I know it's kinda ricey...)<P>I would like to follow a similar testing procedure in the coming weeks.  I<BR>would make base runs and mod runs on the same day.  If the results are very<BR>promising, I might take it to the dyno.  To me, the G-tech is good enough.<BR>If you have not used one, you might want to know that they are quite<BR>consistent when used properly.<P>Now you REALLY know what's going on...<P>Andy<BR>85 V6 SE</B>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||06-04-2001||09:17 PM||||Be interesting if it works.  Can't blame the guy for trying something.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||13
